Bootstrap: docker
From: ubuntu:22.04

# ProtForge GPU container — compute-only artefact for the Snakemake pipeline.
#
# Each Snakemake-submitted SLURM stage invokes this SIF on a GPU compute node
# via `singularity exec --nv ... protforge.sif python <stage_script>`. The
# orchestrator (Snakemake + Streamlit webapp) runs on the host in a small
# Python venv (requirements-host.txt) — it is NOT inside the container.
#
# Why orchestrator-on-host: Snakemake's slurm executor calls `sbatch` directly,
# which inside a Singularity container would need a brittle bind list for the
# Munge socket, /etc/slurm, libslurm, and the slurm binaries. The community
# pattern (GATK, Snakemake, Nextflow on HPC) is uniformly "orchestrator on
# host, compute in container." See Lab/protforge/decisions.md for the full
# rationale and the rejected all-in-one design.
#
# Covers MSA / Boltz / ESM-C / ESMFold. PDAnalysis (ES) is excluded; it
# ships in a separate MPI image.
#
# Bake list:
#   - Tools: boltz, colabfold_search, mmseqs2, esm SDK, transformers
#
# NOT baked (bind-mounted at runtime):
#   - ColabFold MSA databases (~700 GB)  -> /data/colabfold_db
#   - Boltz model checkpoint  (~5 GB)    -> /data/boltz_db
#   - ESM/ESMFold HF cache              -> /models/hf
#   - User input/output dirs            -> rule-level binds (see Snakefile)
#
# Why ubuntu:22.04 and NOT nvidia/cuda as the base:
#   - PyTorch's `cu124` wheels ship their own libcudart / libcublas /
#     libcudnn / libcurand etc. under torch/lib/. Re-installing CUDA at
#     the base layer is ~2 GB of duplicated libraries that PyTorch ignores.
#   - The NVIDIA driver libs (libnvidia-*.so, libcuda.so.1) are injected
#     at runtime by `singularity exec --nv` from the host driver. The
#     container doesn't need its own driver, and we never compile CUDA
#     code at runtime (Triton ships its own ptxas).
#   - Host modules `cuda/...`/`cudnn/...` from the Kempner module system
#     are unused in container mode — they only matter for the bash/conda
#     fallback path (see workflow/rules/*.smk `else` branches).
#   - The non-CUDA base saves ~2 GB of image size and removes the CUDA
#     version pin from a layer that doesn't actually need it.
#
%files
    # Copied last so codebase edits invalidate only the final layer.
    # NOTE: paths are relative to the build context (repo root).
    . /opt/protforge

%post
    set -eux

    # -----------------------------------------------------------------
    # Layer 1: system deps (changes ~never)
    # -----------------------------------------------------------------
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
    apt-get update
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        python3 python3-pip python3-venv \
        git wget curl ca-certificates \
        build-essential cmake \
        libgomp1
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
    ln -sf /usr/bin/python3 /usr/local/bin/python

    # -----------------------------------------------------------------
    # Layer 2: mmseqs2 binary (changes when bumping MMseqs2)
    # -----------------------------------------------------------------
    # GPU build is mandatory — colabfold_search is invoked with `--gpu 1`
    # in workflow/rules/msa.smk; the AVX2 (CPU-only) variant aborts at the
    # prefilter with "MMseqs2 was compiled without CUDA support". The GPU
    # tarball is a strict functional superset (falls back to AVX2 paths
    # when --gpu 0). Discovered in stage-1 E2E baseline 2026-05-28;
    # tracked as audit H0.
    cd /opt
    wget -q https://mmseqs.com/latest/mmseqs-linux-gpu.tar.gz
    tar xzf mmseqs-linux-gpu.tar.gz
    rm mmseqs-linux-gpu.tar.gz
    ln -sf /opt/mmseqs/bin/mmseqs /usr/local/bin/mmseqs

    # -----------------------------------------------------------------
    # Layer 3: Python tools (changes when bumping any tool version)
    # -----------------------------------------------------------------
    pip3 install --no-cache-dir --upgrade pip setuptools wheel

    # PyTorch first (cu124 wheels). All downstream tools pull torch as a dep,
    # so installing it explicitly here pins the version.
    #
    # The cu124 wheels are self-contained: they bundle libcudart/libcublas/
    # libcudnn under torch/lib/. That's why this image starts from
    # ubuntu:22.04 (not nvidia/cuda) — torch carries the CUDA runtime, and
    # `singularity exec --nv` injects the driver libs from the host.
    pip3 install --no-cache-dir \
        torch torchvision torchaudio \
        --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu124

    # Boltz (structure prediction)
    pip3 install --no-cache-dir boltz

    # ESM-C SDK (used by run_esm.py)
    # NB: esm SDK's esm/sdk/base_forge_client.py imports httpx unconditionally
    # even when only the local API is used. pip metadata misses httpx as a dep
    # in some releases — install explicitly to be safe.
    pip3 install --no-cache-dir esm httpx

    # ESMFold via HuggingFace transformers (run_esmfold.py uses EsmForProteinFolding)
    pip3 install --no-cache-dir \
        "transformers>=4.40" accelerate \
        biopython numpy scipy

    # ColabFold (provides colabfold_search; we skip the alphafold/jax extras
    # since we only run the MSA-search side, not structure prediction)
    pip3 install --no-cache-dir \
        "colabfold @ git+https://github.com/sokrypton/ColabFold"

    # ProtForge runtime deps (whatever the scripts import)
    pip3 install --no-cache-dir \
        pyyaml pandas tqdm

    # NB: Snakemake, streamlit, and the slurm executor plugin are NOT installed
    # in the SIF — they live on the host (requirements-host.txt) so the
    # orchestrator can invoke `sbatch` natively. See the top-of-file comment
    # for why, and Lab/protforge/decisions.md for the design history.

    # Pin numpy to the compatible window for boltz + colabfold + numba.
    # - colabfold (current main) declares: numpy>=2.0.2,<3.0
    # - numba (transitive dep of boltz) refuses: NumPy 2.2+ (runtime check)
    # - boltz pyproject declares: numpy>=1.26,<2.0 (DEclARED only; the
    #   actual runtime constraint is numba's, which is satisfied by 2.0.x)
    # Intersection: 2.0.2 <= numpy < 2.1. Force-install so the colabfold
    # step's 2.2.x doesn't stick. Pip will warn about boltz's <2.0 — safe
    # to ignore (the runtime works; the wheel just over-declared).
    pip3 install --no-cache-dir --force-reinstall \
        "numpy>=2.0.2,<2.1"

    # Model weights are intentionally not baked into the image. Create the
    # standard mount points so `singularity exec -B host:/models/hf:ro` works
    # consistently across clusters.
    mkdir -p /models/hf /models/torch /data/protforge

    # -----------------------------------------------------------------
    # Build manifest: a self-describing record of what landed in the SIF.
    # Readable with `singularity exec protforge.sif cat /opt/protforge/container-build-manifest.txt`.
    # Pair this with `singularity inspect $SIF` for the %labels metadata.
    # Each version capture uses `|| echo unknown` so a single missing tool
    # doesn't abort the build (`set -e` is on).
    # -----------------------------------------------------------------
    {
        echo "ProtForge container build manifest"
        echo "===================================="
        echo "Build timestamp: $(date -u +'%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ')"
        echo "Base image:      ubuntu:22.04"
        echo ""
        echo "## Tool versions"
        echo "mmseqs:        $(mmseqs version 2>&1 | head -1 || echo unknown)"
        echo "boltz:         $(boltz --help 2>&1 | head -1 || echo unknown)"
        echo "python:        $(python3 --version 2>&1 || echo unknown)"
        echo ""
        echo "## Python packages (pip freeze)"
        pip3 freeze 2>&1 || echo "(pip freeze failed)"
    } > /opt/protforge/container-build-manifest.txt

%environment
    # ProtForge cache root: host bind-mount target convention.
    # Override at runtime with: singularity exec --env PROTFORGE_HOME=/path ...
    export PROTFORGE_HOME=${PROTFORGE_HOME:-/data/protforge}

    # Marker so application code / Snakefile rules can branch on container
    # mode without sniffing for /opt/protforge etc. Always set inside the SIF.
    export PROTFORGE_CONTAINER=1

    # Default model caches are host bind-mount targets. Populate them with
    # scripts/download_models.py before running compute jobs.
    export HF_HOME=${HF_HOME:-/models/hf}
    export TORCH_HOME=${TORCH_HOME:-/models/torch}

    # Force HF offline mode at runtime. The mounted cache is read-only inside
    # the SIF; offline mode skips network lookups and reads cached files
    # directly. This also turns "cache misconfigured" into a fail-fast error
    # instead of a silent re-download from a compute node (which is forbidden
    # on some Kempner partitions). Override (e.g., for download_models.py
    # invocations) with `--env HF_HUB_OFFLINE=0 --env TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE=0`.
    export HF_HUB_OFFLINE=${HF_HUB_OFFLINE:-1}
    export TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE=${TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE:-1}

    # Bind-mount targets that the rules expect (host paths bound to these):
    #   /data/colabfold_db   <- ColabFold MSA DBs
    #   /data/boltz_db       <- Boltz model checkpoint dir
    #   /models/hf           <- ESM-C / ESMFold HF cache

    # Make sure ProtForge code is importable
    export PYTHONPATH=/opt/protforge${PYTHONPATH:+:$PYTHONPATH}

%runscript
    # Passthrough: forward args to whatever was requested. This is a
    # compute-only artefact — the webapp + Snakemake live on the host and
    # invoke per-stage commands via `singularity exec`, which bypasses
    # %runscript entirely. `singularity run protforge.sif bash` still drops
    # into a shell for debugging.
    #
    # Usage (called by Snakemake, not by hand):
    #   singularity exec --nv protforge-gpu.sif python /opt/protforge/slurm_scripts/run_esm.py ...
    exec "$@"

%labels
    Author thomasbush52@gmail.com
    Version 0.2.0
    Description ProtForge GPU compute container (MSA + Boltz + ESM-C + ESMFold)
    Role compute

%help
    ProtForge GPU container — compute-only artefact for the ProtForge
    Snakemake pipeline. Model weights and databases are bind-mounted at
    runtime; the SIF supplies the Python tools (boltz, colabfold_search,
    mmseqs, esm SDK, transformers).

    The orchestrator (Streamlit webapp + Snakemake + slurm executor plugin)
    runs on the host in a small Python venv — NOT inside this SIF. See
    requirements-host.txt and webapp/connect.sh. Rationale:
    Lab/protforge/decisions.md (sbatch / Munge can't be cleanly bound into
    Singularity; community pattern is orchestrator-on-host).

    GPU model: ubuntu:22.04 base + torch+cu124 wheels (which ship their own
    CUDA runtime). At runtime, `singularity exec --nv` injects the host's
    NVIDIA driver libs. No host `module load cuda/cudnn` is needed in
    container mode — those modules only apply to the bash/conda fallback path.

    Quickstart (invoked by Snakemake, not by hand):
        singularity exec --nv \
            -B /n/holylfs06/.../colabfold/databases:/data/colabfold_db:ro \
            -B /n/holylfs06/.../boltz/boltz_db:/data/boltz_db:ro \
            -B /n/holylfs06/.../protforge/models/hf:/models/hf:ro \
            -B "$PWD":"$PWD" \
            protforge-gpu.sif \
            python /opt/protforge/slurm_scripts/run_esm.py ...

    Build manifest (what got baked in this image):
        singularity exec protforge.sif cat /opt/protforge/container-build-manifest.txt
